<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1739">1739</a>–<a href="/years/1812">1812</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clemens Wenceslaus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> was a son of the Elector of Saxony who became Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1768 and in the same year the last Archbishop-Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>. He fled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> in 1794, and in 1803 his electorate was dissolved. He kept the see of Augsburg until its secularisation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clemens-wenceslaus-of-saxony.jpg" alt="Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Palauenc05 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trier_Stadtmuseum_Clemens_Wenzeslaus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Clemens Wenceslaus was born on 28 September 1739, a prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wettin">House of Wettin</a> and the ninth child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-iii">Augustus III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, who was Prince-Elector of Saxony and King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In 1760 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and entered the Austrian army as a field marshal, and he was present at the Battle of Torgau on 3 November 1760. He decided that warfare was not for him and entered the church instead. In April 1763 he was elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> and Bishop of Regensburg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1764 he served as coadjutor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, and in 1768 he gave up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> and Regensburg, becoming Archbishop-Elector of Trier in February and Prince-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Augsburg in August. In Trier he did much for public education, founded several non-profit bodies for education and the general welfare, and in 1783 issued an edict of tolerance. In church matters he took a mixed course: he let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> remain in Trier after their order was abolished, protested against the radical reforms of his cousin, Emperor Joseph II, and banned several processions and holidays.</p><p>Though a modest man who lived simply, he rebuilt Ehrenbreitstein as a palace and lived there. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in Coblenz, encouraged music in the archdiocese and built a hunting lodge at Kärlich, while opposing several inhumane ways of hunting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> alarmed him. He halted his reforms and began to rule strictly, offered refuge at Schönbornslust palace to members of the French royal family, to which his nephew King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> belonged, and allowed Coblenz to become a centre of French monarchism. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Treaty of Lunéville</a> in 1801 he lost all the electorate's lands west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, keeping only a few small territories belonging to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> itself.</p><p>In 1803 he lost those too, along with the Prince-Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> and the Prince-Provostry of Ellwangen Abbey, which were secularised and annexed by the princes of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a>, the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the Duke of Württemberg. With a pension of 100,000 guldens he retired to Augsburg. He died at the episcopal summer residence in Marktoberdorf in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a> on 27 July 1812 and was buried there. His grandniece Archduchess Maria Clementina of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last elector, <b>Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony</b>, fled, and in 1803 the Reichsdeputationshauptschluss dissolved the electorate, its remaining eastern lands going to Nassau-Weilburg.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-trier">Electorate of Trier</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory was secularised in 1802 and annexed by the Electorate of Bavaria, ending the bishops' secular rule; the last prince-bishop, <b>Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony</b>, was also Elector of Trier.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-augsburg">Prince-Bishopric of Augsburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Marktoberdorf, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.77,10.62&amp;year=1786" aria-label="Marktoberdorf on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4798 490 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Clemens Wenceslaus of Saxony"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5248 760h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5248 760h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.77,10.62&amp;year=1786">What was here in 1786? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
